is faster so we also have things  like for example we work in oncology so  we do a lot of survival analyses and we  have some some kaplan-meier curve  functions that do them in a specific and  standardized way that match our  specifications and actually my colleague  Josh is in the audience and was really  helpful in getting some of those off the  ground  so another thing if anybody has worked  in kind of the biomedical research world  you'll know the the joys and  frustrations of making a table one it's  just basically a table of baseline  characteristics of patients and so  there's nothing too fancy here it's  really looking at just descriptive  statistics about the patient's but you  know you ought to have to do it in a  very specialized format and comparing  the groups and so it's just really nice  to have different kinds of helper  functions to do that and so there's this  really nice package called compare  groups that we use quite a bit and that  allows us to more easily do things that  are specific for this kind of a medical  research environment and so I mentioned  early on that we had some you know  starting to do some work in  collaborations on with companies work  with organizations like the FDA  so early on we had thought actually one  of the reasons to use SAS was there is  this misconception out there that the  FDA only looked at SAS and that they  were against our and you know they had  never actually said that but that's what  people really thought and so one of the  big pieces of pushback we got early on  was oh you can't use our if you ever  want to do anything related to the FDA  well fast forward two years now and  earlier this year we had our first joint  collaborative publication with them come  out and all the analyses we did in our  and it turns out it's really not a big  deal at all and as long as we're using  the right reproducible standards it  hasn't been a problem and a little bit  more about this paper I think because  it's really interesting so this was  actually looking at characteristics of  real-world patients so those are those  ninety-five percent of patients that are  seeing their oncologist but not in a  clinical trial and this is in the  setting of advanced non-small-cell lung  cancer and so looking at those  real-world lung cancer patients compared  to patients in a clinical trial and one  of the things that we found in this  collaboration is that those are very  different types of patients so it's not  just that patients aren't being included  in this research but they're actually  very different in all of these different  ways in patient characteristics and so  what we think is that the studies of  outcomes in these underrepresented  populations through world data are  actually essential for informing  real-world treatment decisions it came  out in January but happy to give you the  reference later so now in 2018 after  this two-year phase this was the  beginning of the year i resurveyed the  team and proficiency with r is now very  high almost everybody rates themselves  at advanced or higher or intermediate  and so i'm not going to necessarily  assume any causality here in terms of  what we did and what happened but i do  think that a lot of the things that we  worked on over time have really helped  with this so are we done have we you  know succeeded is this no more  challenges  well have to tell you now although we  have come so far there are still  challenges that remain and next level  success looks different than what we've  done in the past so early on success was  things like deciding we're gonna use our  and getting sweatshirts now we have what  I think are kind of if I think of them  as grown-up problems to have so these  are the ones that are you know harder to  solve a little bit less fun but our  serious problem is when you get to a  more mature and more scale team and so  going forward for the next two years a  lot of the things that really are really  focusing on now is thinking about  balancing independence with  standardization so the independence  thing is is really twofold so from the  perspective of moving quickly we like to  have teams that can work independently  and it can do things that the way they  want to and just move fast also a lot of  people on the team coming from academic  backgrounds and I think that's really  ingrained and if who we are and how we  think about ourself is no one tells me  how to write my code no one tells me  which packages to use on the other hand  there are a lot of benefits of  standardization both in terms of the  packages that you use and the style that  you write in some of the best practices  and so I think trying to find that  balance of how much specification and  standardization do you need to have you  know we have a draft style guide but not  everybody follows it how much do we have  force it those are some of the questions  that we're tackling right now one of the  biggest questions is how do we find time  to devote to infrastructure and balance  the opportunity cost because we are  growing so quickly every hour that we  spend on thinking about planning and  infrastructure is an hour that we take  away from doing something new and cool  and exciting but as you know it's not  really sustainable to only do those new  cool exciting things if you don't think  about investing and spending down on  technical debt and you know we actually  a few months ago when I did this survey  I asked what is the biggest barrier to  you for contributing more to  our internal package or any of our other  infrastructure or coding culture and the  number one answer it wasn't lack of  desire it wasn't lack of skills or  restrictions and the functions it was  time people want to contribute we just  need to find how to balance the time and  how to protect that so another thing is  thinking about versioning and  maintenance of an internal art package  so once you have a package that's great  but then who is the owner of that  package how do you think about  maintaining it and especially when  you're thinking about working across  multiple teams where the ownership is is  less clear and not everybody has the  same amount of time to devote to it how  do you make that so that's fair and it  also is being maintained and then also I  mentioned earlier a lot of those  stickers that I had up were for tidy  verse packages so we loved them they're  great but should we require people to  use them this is the debate I see in  code reviews all the time going back and  forth someone says oh you should use you  know some tidy verse package here and  suppose like well I'd really like to do  that in base R and so they go back and  forth and it's really hard to know how  much to just encourage verses to enforce  there and so this next phase of growth  so about two months ago my colleague  caught TNI we're thinking about this  with some other folks on the team and we  said you know we are really at a  decision point now we really need to  think about what this next phase of  growth is and change the strategies for  how we figure this out and how we plan  about this going forward so we had a  meeting with the team and we presented  the problem in the motivation so the the  agenda was we talked about some of these  goals that we had then and the goals  that we have now and how they're very  different and need different solutions  and what some of the challenges we face  are and so we did that to get people to  acknowledge that there were new  challenges that we had to address and to  get buy-in and also to get people  excited about what could be the next  stage of this and how to actually be  part of that and so that you know two  years from now if I'm giving a talk  again that they could actually see some  of the work that they did  part of the solution there so we started  a new working group CAUTI my colleague  did and then got started on on planning  and thinking about how where to go from  here and so the first things that this  group has done in the last few months is  starting off with the first set of goals  of defining things like coding standards  and best practices and also it's not  enough to define best practices but  think about how we should hold ourselves  accountable to them and if they should  be enforced and one of the first  standards that they came up with was  thinking about coding readability and  kind of a simple guide of writing their  code with an audience in mind one of the  things that came up often is that the  code that one might write for an  analysis is very different than a code  and a software engineer might write for  something in production and thinking  about actually telling the story laying  out the assumptions thinking about all  of the things that go into the analysis  as part of how they write the code and  next is thinking about these best  practices you know how do you actually  integrate those into culture so it's not  like you're just telling someone to do  something but it's actually part of  something that people want to do and you  know the last we're gonna get some new  stickers because that is really  motivating and so rather than the  stickers we had before that everybody  got and I think this this idea might  have come from the Airbnb data science  team or another data science team I'm  sure of actually only giving them out to  people who are contributing certain  things and the the core group so to have  that kind of motivation and you know  thinking about there's a lot of  challenges around package and  environment management that are gonna be  coming up in the future that we want to  tackle and so just want to thank  everybody on both the team the current  past and future members and in  particular this working group that my  colleague caught - you just spun up and  that's thinking about some of these  next-level grown-up challenges and also  our other collaborators in the art  community and then just a shameless plug  that we are still growing we might  double in size again so please get in  touch with me if you're interested in  joining thank you  you  you
 

